:name: SX1262 over the radio model
:description: Drives the Renode peripheral directly, before any firmware depends on it.
:
: The peripheral is a wire to radioserver rather than a model of a chip, so what
: is under test here is the wire: chip select framing, byte order, and that the
: answers coming back are the ones VirtualSX1262 gives a native node.
:
: Start the radio model first, on a TCP port, with no engine attached:
:   radioserver :7777
:
: Then:
:   renode tools/renode/peripherals/radioserver_test.resc

mach create "radio"
machine LoadPlatformDescription @tools/renode/peripherals/radioserver.repl

radio Connect

: GetStatus (0xC0) then one byte clocked out to read the answer. A chip in
: standby answers 0x20 - mode 2 in bits 6:4 - and a chip that is not there
: answers 0x00, which is how "no radio model" looks from the firmware's side.
echo "GetStatus, expect 0x2x:"
radio Transmit 0xC0
radio Transmit 0x00

: WriteBuffer at offset 0, three bytes, then read them back. This is the one
: that caught the earlier model out: the buffer commands are variable length and
: run until chip select deasserts, so a model treating them as fixed length
: parses payload as opcodes and returns garbage after the first byte.
echo "WriteBuffer AA BB CC:"
radio Transmit 0x0E
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0xAA
radio Transmit 0xBB
radio Transmit 0xCC
radio FinishTransmission

echo "ReadBuffer, expect AA BB CC:"
radio Transmit 0x1E
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio Transmit 0x00
radio FinishTransmission
